{
  "concept": "As a shallow neural network's hidden-unit count grows, its fitted function does not become wilder — it passes through the same training points while becoming steadily smoother and closer to the true underlying function.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "3-column × 2-row grid of six panels labelled a) b) c) d) e) f)",
    "Shared x-axis for every panel: 'Input, x', ticks at 0.0, 0.5, 1.0",
    "Shared y-axis for every panel: 'Output, y', ticks at -1.0, 0.0, 1.0",
    "Panel titles: a) has no title (ground-truth panel), b) '6 hidden units', c) '7 hidden units', d) '8 hidden units', e) '10 hidden units', f) '50 hidden units'",
    "Black thick curve in every panel: the true generating function — a smooth wave rising from about -0.3 at x=0 to a peak near +0.6 at x≈0.26, falling to a trough near -0.65 at x≈0.75, then rising slightly to about -0.3 at x=1",
    "Seven salmon/terracotta filled circular markers: the training data, clustered at small x (≈0.03, 0.12, 0.19) and large x (≈0.81, 0.91, 0.97) with a visible gap of no data in the middle of the domain",
    "Pale teal/mint thick curve in panels b–f: the piecewise-linear fitted network output, always passing exactly through the seven data points",
    "Panel b (6 units): fit shoots far off-scale (clipped at y = ±1) across the data-free middle region",
    "Panel c (7 units): fit overshoots to ≈0.9 near x=0.28 and plunges below -1 near x=0.72",
    "Panel d (8 units): fit still overshoots (≈0.75 peak, ≈-0.85 trough) but stays on-scale",
    "Panel e (10 units): fit tracks the black curve closely with mild kinks",
    "Panel f (50 units): fit nearly coincides with the black curve, visibly smooth",
    "Legend: black = true function, teal = fitted network, orange dots = training data",
    "Annotation marking the data-free middle region where the fits differ most"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "hiddenUnits",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Sweeping capacity shows the fit going from wildly divergent in the unconstrained middle region to smoothly matching the true function — extra parameters buy smoothness, not chaos.",
      "label": "Hidden units",
      "range": [0, 4, 1],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "layout",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Seeing all six panels together makes the monotone trend obvious; zooming to one panel lets the learner check the fit against individual data points.",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["All six panels", "Single panel"],
      "default": "All six panels"
    },
    {
      "id": "panelSelect",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Clicking a panel in the grid focuses it, revealing how exactly the teal fit interpolates each orange data point at that capacity."
    },
    {
      "id": "overlay6vs50",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Superimposing the 6-unit and 50-unit fits on the same axes contrasts a barely-sufficient model that flails between data points with a hugely over-parameterised one that hugs the truth.",
      "label": "Overlay 6 vs 50 units",
      "default": false
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The target and the data",
      "narration": "Panel a) shows the true function (black) and the seven training points (orange). Notice the points cluster at the two ends — the middle of the input range has no data at all.",
      "state": { "layout": "All six panels", "hiddenUnits": 0, "overlay6vs50": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Just enough capacity: 6 units",
      "narration": "With 6 hidden units the network can still pass through every data point, but in the data-free middle it swings completely off the chart. The fit is not wrong at the data — it is unconstrained away from it.",
      "state": { "layout": "Single panel", "hiddenUnits": 0, "overlay6vs50": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Adding units: 7 and 8",
      "narration": "One or two extra units pull the excursions back on-scale, but the curve still overshoots the true peak and trough. More parameters are already making the function tamer, not wilder.",
      "state": { "layout": "Single panel", "hiddenUnits": 2, "overlay6vs50": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Plenty of capacity: 10 units",
      "narration": "At 10 units the piecewise-linear fit tracks the black curve across the whole domain, with only small kinks where the joints between linear regions sit.",
      "state": { "layout": "Single panel", "hiddenUnits": 3, "overlay6vs50": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Massive capacity: 50 units",
      "narration": "With 50 hidden units — far more parameters than data points — the fit is the smoothest of all and nearly indistinguishable from the true function. The expected 'overfitting explosion' never arrives.",
      "state": { "layout": "Single panel", "hiddenUnits": 4, "overlay6vs50": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Rough versus smooth, side by side",
      "narration": "Overlay the 6-unit and 50-unit solutions: both interpolate the same seven points, yet only the over-parameterised one behaves sensibly where there is no data.",
      "state": { "layout": "Single panel", "hiddenUnits": 4, "overlay6vs50": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "The whole trend",
      "narration": "Back in the grid, read b) → f) as a single sequence: increasing capacity monotonically increases smoothness and fidelity to the truth.",
      "state": { "layout": "All six panels", "hiddenUnits": 4, "overlay6vs50": false }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Over-parameterisation does not make a network wigglier — every one of these models fits the seven points exactly, but the ones with far more hidden units than data choose the smoothest such function and therefore generalise best.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate as a 3×2 grid of small line plots on white, thin black axis frames with ticks only on the left and bottom of each panel. Every panel shares x ∈ [0,1] (ticks 0.0, 0.5, 1.0, label 'Input, x' under the bottom row) and y ∈ [-1,1] (ticks -1.0, 0.0, 1.0, label 'Output, y' on the left column). The true function is a heavy black smooth wave: starts ≈-0.3, peaks ≈+0.6 around x≈0.26, troughs ≈-0.65 around x≈0.75, ends ≈-0.3. Seven large salmon (#E08A66) filled dots sit on or near that curve, three bunched near x<0.2 and three near x>0.8, plus one at x≈0.81 below the curve — leaving an obvious empty middle. Fits are thick pale-teal (#A8DBD2) piecewise-linear polylines drawn on top of, but behind, the dots; they always pass through the dots and differ only between them. Panel a) has no teal curve and no title; panels b–f are titled '6/7/8/10/50 hidden units'. Clip panel b's and c's excursions at the y-limits so the divergence reads as 'off the chart'. Serif-free bold panel letters a)–f) at the top-left of each panel. In single-panel mode, enlarge the selected panel, keep the same colors, and add a faint shaded band over the data-free middle region with the note 'no training data here'."
}

